WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.395
Lesson 0.1.

00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:05.362
Money, ledgers and why blockchains exist.

00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:07.676
By the end, you'll be able to explain,

00:00:07.676 --> 00:00:10.228
in plain words, what a blockchain is, what

00:00:10.228 --> 00:00:13.016
a cryptocurrency is, and what DeFi means, well

00:00:13.016 --> 00:00:14.737
enough to teach someone else.

00:00:16.037 --> 00:00:17.327
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.327 --> 00:00:19.623
First, money as a record, and why a

00:00:19.623 --> 00:00:22.792
blockchain is a record nobody controls alone.

00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:25.406
Second, what Bitcoin and Ethereum actually

00:00:25.406 --> 00:00:27.226
are, with the real dates.

00:00:27.226 --> 00:00:30.651
Third, smart contracts, stablecoins and DeFi.

00:00:30.651 --> 00:00:34.246
And finally, a full worked example: Alice

00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:37.071
sends Bob 0.1 ETH, step by step,

00:00:37.071 --> 00:00:39.126
on a real wallet screen.

00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:42.650
One honest note before we start.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.095
Some of the screens in this

00:00:44.095 --> 00:00:46.510
lesson are clearly labelled illustrative.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.991
They're accurate recreations of what you'll see, built

00:00:49.991 --> 00:00:52.079
so we can show you the idea, not

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:54.484
a live capture of any one exchange or

00:00:54.484 --> 00:00:58.075
wallet, whose exact design changes over time anyway.

00:00:58.075 --> 00:01:00.816
When you get to Lesson 0.2, you'll be looking

00:01:00.816 --> 00:01:03.080
at the real thing, on your own screen.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.540
Money is a record.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:07.900
Start here.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.310
Money is a record of who owns what.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.388
Your bank balance isn't a pile of cash

00:01:12.388 --> 00:01:14.765
sitting somewhere with your name on it.

00:01:14.765 --> 00:01:16.675
It's a line in your bank's private

00:01:16.675 --> 00:01:18.695
ledger, a record book, and you trust

00:01:18.695 --> 00:01:20.605
the bank to keep that line correct.

00:01:21.905 --> 00:01:23.298
Here's what happens when you

00:01:23.298 --> 00:01:24.895
pay someone through a bank.

00:01:24.895 --> 00:01:26.800
You tell the bank to move money.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.545
The bank checks your balance.

00:01:28.545 --> 00:01:30.561
It edits its private ledger: minus

00:01:30.561 --> 00:01:32.185
from you, plus to them.

00:01:32.185 --> 00:01:33.267
And it can, if it

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:35.250
needs to, reverse that edit.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.260
A chargeback, a fraud reversal, a correction.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.868
The bank is the one party everyone trusts

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:43.475
to keep, and if needed unwind, the record.

00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:47.130
A blockchain asks a different question.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.786
What if, instead of one company keeping the

00:01:49.786 --> 00:01:53.166
ledger, thousands of independent computers each kept an

00:01:53.166 --> 00:01:56.785
identical copy, and checked every new entry together?

00:01:56.785 --> 00:01:59.170
That's a blockchain: a shared ledger.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.233
New entries can't be quietly changed, because every

00:02:02.233 --> 00:02:04.890
other copy would disagree and reject it.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.747
The trade-off is real, and it's the whole reason

00:02:07.747 --> 00:02:10.605
this program starts with safety: there's no undo.

00:02:11.905 --> 00:02:13.440
Picture it concretely.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.474
One node tries to edit its copy

00:02:15.474 --> 00:02:17.745
to say it received extra coins.

00:02:17.745 --> 00:02:20.395
Every other node compares notes, sees the

00:02:20.395 --> 00:02:23.535
mismatch, and simply ignores the edited copy.

00:02:23.535 --> 00:02:25.543
To fake an entry, you'd have to

00:02:25.543 --> 00:02:27.865
control most of the network at once,

00:02:27.865 --> 00:02:30.675
which is enormously expensive by design.

00:02:30.675 --> 00:02:33.465
No single company decides what happened.

00:02:33.465 --> 00:02:35.903
And that's also why no one, not even the

00:02:35.903 --> 00:02:38.460
network itself, can undo a payment for you.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.821
One real number, so “thousands of

00:02:41.821 --> 00:02:44.000
computers” isn't just a phrase.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.691
Ethereum's ledger is checked by roughly 1

00:02:46.691 --> 00:02:50.471
million independent validators worldwide, each one holding

00:02:50.471 --> 00:02:52.885
a copy and voting on what's true.

00:02:52.885 --> 00:02:55.410
That's not a company with a data centre.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.264
It's closer to a million strangers, on every

00:02:58.264 --> 00:03:00.800
continent, refusing to agree with a lie.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.945
New entries arrive in batches called blocks.

00:03:04.945 --> 00:03:07.363
Each block carries a seal, a fingerprint

00:03:07.363 --> 00:03:09.781
built from everything inside it plus the

00:03:09.781 --> 00:03:11.555
seal of the block before.

00:03:11.555 --> 00:03:13.630
That links the blocks into a chain.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.835
Change one old entry, and its seal

00:03:15.835 --> 00:03:18.780
changes, which breaks every seal after it.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.205
Every other copy notices immediately.

00:03:21.205 --> 00:03:23.463
That's why a blockchain's history can't

00:03:23.463 --> 00:03:25.495
be quietly rewritten, only added to.

00:03:26.795 --> 00:03:29.380
And because it's public, anyone can check it.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.801
This is what a block explorer shows: an

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:34.887
illustrative recreation of the kind of tool you'll

00:03:34.887 --> 00:03:37.305
actually use later in this program.

00:03:37.305 --> 00:03:40.080
A block number, how many transactions it holds,

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.865
and each one's sender, receiver and amount.

00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:45.145
It can show you a transaction happened.

00:03:45.145 --> 00:03:46.610
It can never undo one.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.340
Bitcoin and Ethereum.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.488
A cryptocurrency is simply a

00:03:52.488 --> 00:03:54.700
unit recorded on a blockchain.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.505
That's the whole definition.

00:03:56.505 --> 00:03:59.674
What makes Bitcoin and Ethereum different from each other

00:03:59.674 --> 00:04:01.860
is what each blockchain was built to do.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.620
Here's the real timeline, not a simplified version.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.420
In 2008, someone using the name Satoshi

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.920
Nakamoto published the Bitcoin white paper: money

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.110
with no bank in the middle.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:18.300
In 2009, the first block was mined, the

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.165
first working blockchain, and the first cryptocurrency, BTC.

00:04:23.165 --> 00:04:27.232
In 2015, Ethereum launched: a blockchain that also

00:04:27.232 --> 00:04:30.642
runs small programs, not just payments.

00:04:30.642 --> 00:04:33.399
And through the 2020s, DeFi grew up on

00:04:33.399 --> 00:04:37.005
top of that: lending, trading and stablecoins, run

00:04:37.005 --> 00:04:39.267
by those programs at real scale.

00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:42.207
So, side by side.

00:04:42.207 --> 00:04:44.869
Bitcoin does one thing and does it well: it

00:04:44.869 --> 00:04:48.136
moves and stores value, with the deepest security and

00:04:48.136 --> 00:04:51.037
the longest track record of any blockchain.

00:04:51.037 --> 00:04:53.420
Ethereum does that too, but it also

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.199
runs smart contracts, which is what makes

00:04:56.199 --> 00:04:59.742
lending apps, exchanges and stablecoins possible.

00:04:59.742 --> 00:05:02.319
Most of this program happens on Ethereum

00:05:02.319 --> 00:05:04.959
and the networks built around it, because

00:05:04.959 --> 00:05:07.033
that's where DeFi actually lives.

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:11.938
One more real number, so “gas” isn't abstract later.

00:05:11.938 --> 00:05:16.377
Ethereum processes somewhere around 1.2 million transactions on

00:05:16.377 --> 00:05:19.152
an average day, each one checked by the

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.463
network and added to a block.

00:05:21.463 --> 00:05:24.400
That volume is exactly why block space is

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.547
scarce, and why, in Lesson 0.6, you'll learn

00:05:27.547 --> 00:05:30.903
that transaction fees rise and fall with demand.

00:05:32.203 --> 00:05:33.293
Quick check.

00:05:33.293 --> 00:05:35.620
What's the one core difference between what

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.523
Bitcoin does and what Ethereum does?

00:05:41.943 --> 00:05:44.888
The answer: Bitcoin moves and stores value.

00:05:44.888 --> 00:05:47.610
Ethereum does that too, but it also runs

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:51.328
smart contracts, small programs that make DeFi possible.

00:05:52.629 --> 00:05:54.499
Contracts, coins and DeFi.

00:05:55.799 --> 00:05:58.369
A smart contract is a program that lives

00:05:58.369 --> 00:06:01.176
on a blockchain and follows fixed rules.

00:06:01.176 --> 00:06:02.861
Picture a vending machine.

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:05.351
You insert coins, the rules decide what

00:06:05.351 --> 00:06:08.266
happens, and you get the result instantly.

00:06:08.266 --> 00:06:10.185
There's no cashier: if you press

00:06:10.185 --> 00:06:12.636
the wrong button, nobody refunds you.

00:06:12.636 --> 00:06:16.126
A lending app's smart contract might say, in effect,

00:06:16.126 --> 00:06:19.615
“whoever deposits X gets Y back, plus interest,” and

00:06:19.615 --> 00:06:22.776
it will do exactly that, every time, for anyone.

00:06:24.076 --> 00:06:27.471
A stablecoin is a token designed to stay worth

00:06:27.471 --> 00:06:30.794
about one dollar, like USDC or USDT. Think of

00:06:30.794 --> 00:06:34.334
it as a digital dollar voucher: the issuer holds

00:06:34.334 --> 00:06:37.874
roughly a dollar in reserve for every token, and

00:06:37.874 --> 00:06:41.136
you can swap the token back for a dollar.

00:06:41.136 --> 00:06:44.161
You'll use stablecoins constantly in this program.

00:06:44.161 --> 00:06:47.108
But notice the word “designed”: a stablecoin is

00:06:47.108 --> 00:06:49.379
only as good as its reserves and its

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:51.521
issuer, and it can still fail.

00:06:51.521 --> 00:06:54.439
You'll learn how to judge that in Lesson 1.5.

00:06:55.739 --> 00:06:58.462
Put those two ideas together and you get

00:06:58.462 --> 00:07:03.443
DeFi, decentralised finance: financial services, trading, lending, earning

00:07:03.443 --> 00:07:07.063
interest, run by smart contracts instead of banks.

00:07:07.063 --> 00:07:08.813
Take an ordinary trade.

00:07:08.813 --> 00:07:10.823
On a bank's system, it goes through

00:07:10.823 --> 00:07:13.056
the bank's own systems and staff before

00:07:13.056 --> 00:07:14.843
it settles in the bank's ledger.

00:07:16.143 --> 00:07:18.768
The same trade in DeFi skips all of that.

00:07:18.768 --> 00:07:20.950
It goes straight into a smart contract,

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:23.268
and settles on the blockchain itself.

00:07:23.268 --> 00:07:25.741
No branch, no staff, no opening hours,

00:07:25.741 --> 00:07:27.833
just the contract and the ledger.

00:07:29.133 --> 00:07:32.618
So here's DeFi against traditional finance, directly.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:34.986
A bank has a branch, staff and opening

00:07:34.986 --> 00:07:38.018
hours, and it can reverse a mistaken payment.

00:07:38.018 --> 00:07:40.329
A DeFi smart contract has none of

00:07:40.329 --> 00:07:42.776
that: it's open all day, every day,

00:07:42.776 --> 00:07:46.338
needs no staff, and treats everyone identically.

00:07:46.338 --> 00:07:48.933
But it also can't make an exception for you.

00:07:48.933 --> 00:07:49.823
That's the deal.

00:07:51.123 --> 00:07:53.972
And here's why DeFi doesn't need banking hours.

00:07:53.972 --> 00:07:56.425
A bank transfer can sit for a business day

00:07:56.425 --> 00:07:59.520
or more, waiting on staff and settlement windows.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.634
A new block on Ethereum settles roughly every

00:08:02.634 --> 00:08:05.980
12 seconds, day or night, weekend or holiday.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.255
Look at that gap.

00:08:07.255 --> 00:08:10.020
DeFi isn't faster because it's magic.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.490
It's faster because there's no one to wait on.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.513
Which brings us to the trade-off

00:08:15.513 --> 00:08:17.745
underneath everything in this module.

00:08:17.745 --> 00:08:19.684
No bank in the middle means nobody

00:08:19.684 --> 00:08:22.200
can freeze your money without your keys.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.500
It also means nobody can reverse your mistakes.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.484
That single sentence is why this

00:08:27.484 --> 00:08:29.950
program starts with safety, not strategy.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:32.255
Worked example.

00:08:33.555 --> 00:08:36.340
Here's Alice's wallet, right before she sends.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:39.862
This is a labelled, illustrative recreation of a typical

00:08:39.862 --> 00:08:43.075
wallet screen, built to show the real fields you'll

00:08:43.075 --> 00:08:45.723
see, not a capture of any one product.

00:08:45.723 --> 00:08:48.651
She fills in Bob's address, an amount,

00:08:48.651 --> 00:08:51.279
0.1 ETH, and sees the network fee,

00:08:51.279 --> 00:08:54.658
the gas, that this transaction will cost.

00:08:54.658 --> 00:08:56.283
When she taps Sign and Send,

00:08:56.283 --> 00:08:57.983
she isn't sending a file.

00:08:57.983 --> 00:09:01.177
She's signing an instruction: “move 0.1

00:09:01.177 --> 00:09:03.572
ETH from my address to Bob's.”

00:09:04.872 --> 00:09:07.147
Now follow the instruction itself.

00:09:07.147 --> 00:09:10.191
Alice's wallet signs it with her private key, which

00:09:10.191 --> 00:09:13.652
proves it's really her without ever revealing the key.

00:09:13.652 --> 00:09:17.053
The network checks that she actually owns 0.1

00:09:17.053 --> 00:09:19.867
ETH. It's added to the next block.

00:09:19.867 --> 00:09:22.658
And every copy of the ledger updates at once.

00:09:22.658 --> 00:09:24.143
Bob now owns it.

00:09:24.143 --> 00:09:26.788
There is no undo button, anywhere in this chain.

00:09:28.088 --> 00:09:30.853
And here's the same transaction, now that it's

00:09:30.853 --> 00:09:34.153
settled, on that illustrative block explorer again.

00:09:34.153 --> 00:09:38.051
Anyone, including Bob, including Alice, including a

00:09:38.051 --> 00:09:40.974
stranger, can look it up: from Alice's

00:09:40.974 --> 00:09:44.123
address, to Bob's, 0.1 ETH, confirmed.

00:09:44.123 --> 00:09:45.831
Nothing about it is private, and

00:09:45.831 --> 00:09:47.538
nothing about it can be reversed.

00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:49.928
Quick check.

00:09:49.928 --> 00:09:53.283
When Alice sends Bob crypto, what exactly does she

00:09:57.703 --> 00:10:01.186
sign? [[pause 4]] The answer: an instruction, something

00:10:01.186 --> 00:10:03.549
like “move 0.1 ETH from my address to

00:10:03.549 --> 00:10:06.037
Bob's.” She never sends a file, and she

00:10:06.037 --> 00:10:07.903
never reveals her private key.

00:10:09.203 --> 00:10:10.663
Here's your checklist.

00:10:10.663 --> 00:10:12.198
Do it now, for real.

00:10:12.198 --> 00:10:15.696
First, you can explain a blockchain as “a shared

00:10:15.696 --> 00:10:19.551
ledger nobody can quietly edit.” Second, you know the

00:10:19.551 --> 00:10:23.063
difference between BTC, ETH and a stablecoin.

00:10:23.063 --> 00:10:24.860
And third, you understand that

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:27.063
crypto transactions can't be reversed.

00:10:28.363 --> 00:10:29.573
Question one.

00:10:29.573 --> 00:10:32.678
Why can't someone quietly change an old blockchain entry?

00:10:37.098 --> 00:10:40.207
The answer: thousands of computers hold copies, and

00:10:40.207 --> 00:10:42.837
would reject any version that doesn't match.

00:10:44.137 --> 00:10:46.192
Two more from the checklist, quickly.

00:10:46.192 --> 00:10:48.627
What is a stablecoin designed to do?

00:10:48.627 --> 00:10:50.607
Stay worth about $1, or another

00:10:50.607 --> 00:10:52.887
currency, and it can still fail.

00:10:52.887 --> 00:10:54.576
And what does DeFi replace, and

00:10:54.576 --> 00:10:56.052
what do you give up?

00:10:56.052 --> 00:10:59.254
It replaces banks and brokers with smart contracts,

00:10:59.254 --> 00:11:01.655
and you give up anyone who can reverse

00:11:01.655 --> 00:11:03.872
a mistake or help you recover funds.

00:11:05.172 --> 00:11:06.417
Let's recap.

00:11:06.417 --> 00:11:08.827
Money is a record of who owns what.

00:11:08.827 --> 00:11:11.231
A blockchain is a ledger that thousands

00:11:11.231 --> 00:11:13.514
of computers keep together, so no old

00:11:13.514 --> 00:11:15.557
entry can be quietly changed.

00:11:15.557 --> 00:11:19.487
Bitcoin, from 2009, moves and stores value.

00:11:19.487 --> 00:11:24.305
Ethereum, from 2015, does that and runs smart contracts.

00:11:24.305 --> 00:11:26.605
Stablecoins aim to hold a dollar.

00:11:26.605 --> 00:11:30.195
And DeFi is financial services run by those contracts,

00:11:30.195 --> 00:11:33.655
with the trade-off that nobody can reverse a mistake.

00:11:34.955 --> 00:11:37.470
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:39.743
Next up, Lesson 0.2: Opening

00:11:39.743 --> 00:11:42.330
and securing an exchange account.
